            <ArticleTitle>Fetal pain, abortion, viability, and the Constitution.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>235-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1748-720X.2011.00592.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In early 2010, the Nebraska state legislature passed a new abortion restricting law asserting a new, compelling state interest in preventing fetal pain. In this article, we review existing constitutional abortion doctrine and note difficulties presented by persistent legal attention to a socially derived viability construct. We then offer a substantive biological, ethical, and legal critique of the new fetal pain rationale.</AbstractText>
